// Title: PowerShell Get-Process LSASS
// Sigma ID: b2815d0d-7481-4bf0-9b6c-a4c48a94b349
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2021-04-23 | Modified: 2023-01-05
// ATT&CK: credential-access, T1552.004
// Description: Detects a "Get-Process" cmdlet and it's aliases on lsass process, which is in almost all cases a
//   sign of malicious activity
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://web.archive.org/web/20220205033028/https://twitter.com/PythonResponder/status/1385064506049630211
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_powershell_getprocess_lsass.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "Get-Process lsas" or ProcessCommandLine contains "ps lsas" or ProcessCommandLine contains "gps lsas"
